From: atomchild411 <143453386+atomchild411@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 22:30:00 -0900
Subject: [PATCH] WTF: IRIX threads bound to their own kernel vehicle, 1 MB stacks

On a VPro Octane2 the kernel kept killing WPEWebProcess with "process or
stack limit exceeded" a few seconds into a page load or a new tab (two
kills in a five-step browsing scenario). It never happened under dbx or
par; par showed libpthread's preemption signal, SIGPTRESCHED (48),
arriving many times in the Web process. Larger thread stacks (8 MB) did
not help.

IRIX threads default to process scope: many threads share a few kernel
execution vehicles and libpthread switches them around with that signal.
Creating WebKit's threads with PTHREAD_SCOPE_BOUND_NP gives each its own
vehicle, scheduled by the kernel and never switched; it needs no
privilege (unlike PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM). With every thread bound the same
scenario ran twice without a kill, pages drawn correctly.

Threads with no stack size of their own also get 1 MB instead of IRIX's
128 KB; other systems give WebKit's threads megabytes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/posix/ThreadingPOSIX.cpp
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/posix/ThreadingPOSIX.cpp
@@ -314,6 +314,21 @@
 #if HAVE(SCHEDULING_POLICIES)
     pthread_attr_setschedpolicy(&attr, schedPolicy(schedulingPolicy));
 #endif
+#if OS(IRIX)
+    // In its default (process) scope an IRIX thread shares a few kernel
+    // execution vehicles with the process's other threads, and libpthread
+    // moves it between them with a signal (SIGPTRESCHED). Under WebKit's load
+    // the kernel then kills processes with "process or stack limit exceeded".
+    // A bound thread has a vehicle of its own, needs no privilege, and is never
+    // moved. IRIX's default stack (128 KB) is also far below what WebKit's
+    // threads get elsewhere.
+#ifndef PTHREAD_SCOPE_BOUND_NP
+#define PTHREAD_SCOPE_BOUND_NP 2
+#endif
+    pthread_attr_setscope(&attr, PTHREAD_SCOPE_BOUND_NP);
+    if (!stackSize)
+        stackSize = 1 * MB;
+#endif
     if (stackSize)
         pthread_attr_setstacksize(&attr, stackSize.value());
     int error = pthread_create(&threadHandle, &attr, wtfThreadEntryPoint, &context);
